+## Local workspace and Epsilon sign-in
+
+With the workbench-enabled SDK installed, run this from the project directory:
+
+```bash
+epsilon start
+```
+
+Choose **Sign in with Epsilon**. The browser opens Epsilon's existing sign-in
+service, reuses your Epsilon session when available, and returns to the local
+workspace. The SDK does not ask for your password in its UI. Account tokens stay
+in the SDK's protected local credentials file and are shared with CLI commands.
+
+The local app uses authorization code flow with PKCE. The platform's public
+`sdk-client` must register `http://127.0.0.1/auth/callback` and
+`http://localhost/auth/callback` with PKCE `S256` required. Keycloak supports the
+actual local port when matching these loopback registrations. No client secret
+or wildcard web origin is needed in this template.
+
+The workbench is currently on an unreleased SDK branch. For development, install
+that checkout in this template's Python environment rather than relying on the
+older PyPI package:
+
+```bash
+pip install -e '/absolute/path/to/sdk-epsilon[workbench,copilot]'
+```
+
+For an institution's own identity provider, set `EPSILON_SSO_ISSUER` and, if
+different, `EPSILON_SSO_CLIENT_ID` and `EPSILON_SSO_AUDIENCE` before starting the
+SDK. Use HTTPS for remote identity services; HTTP is accepted only on loopback.
+The production defaults are the Epsilon realm at
+`https://app.epsilon-data.org/keycloak/realms/epsilon`, client `sdk-client` and
+audience `epsilon.api`. A changed API server requires a matching sign-in.
+
+Signing out of the SDK clears its local account tokens and preserves project
+files. It does not sign you out of every Epsilon website. The SDK refresh token
+belongs to the ordinary Keycloak session; central sign-out prevents later
+refresh, while an already issued access token may remain valid until expiry.
